WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.560
Can a French person understand a Quebec
French speaker from Canada? Apparently not,

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:09.520
according to the many comments on my last video.
I'm French, I live in Paris, and today I want to

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:14.480
react to a few videos of people speaking Quebec
French and see how much of it I can actually

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:19.520
understand. So if you've never heard Quebec French
before, it is pretty different from the French

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:24.800
spoken in France. And you need to know that French
is the only official language of Quebec in Canada,

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:29.520
and it's because back in the 17th century,
Canada was a French colony until it was lost

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:34.800
to Britain in 1763. French speakers there
became surrounded with English speakers,

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.440
but they held on tightly to their culture
and their language, which evolved totally

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:43.360
separately from the French from France.
And as a French person, one of the questions

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:48.240
I had when researching this video is: how is
Quebec keeping their language alive when there

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:52.955
are so many English speakers around them? And
to understand that, let's watch our first video.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:06.960
First of all, I want to say that I
understand everyone very well. Like,

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.880
there's over 8 million people in the province
of Quebec, so it's one of the most well-known

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:14.640
ways of speaking French. Everyone who
just spoke here has a very understandable

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:18.479
accent to me as a French person, even though
you'll see there's quite a few differences.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.080
So that's really interesting to
me — that especially in Montreal,

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:46.480
more and more people have to speak English
at work. And I have found in my research

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:51.120
that there's a lot of talk on how to keep the
French language alive. And you know, I also

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:54.800
relate to what this girl said, because
it's not about refusing English,

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:58.334
but it's more about keeping your culture,
your language, and your identity.

00:02:07.301 --> 00:02:11.520
It feels like there's a big gap between
Montreal and the rest of the province. Like,

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:18.480
66% versus 84% is quite a big gap. And since we're
on the topic of the rest of Quebec, let's now make

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:23.468
it harder for me and let's try to understand a
Quebec accent of someone who's not from Montreal.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:34.960
All right, this is very hard for me. I get the
context — you know, he's a fisherman out of the

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:40.000
beautiful Îles de la Madeleine — but I don't
understand too much. So let's put subtitles on.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:59.600
It is tough, but I feel if I train my ears,
I would 100% understand him. And I don't know

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:04.880
about you, but I feel his accent reminds me
of the Cajun French speakers from Louisiana,

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:08.240
which I already made a video about.
And it's so interesting to me to see

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:11.600
how many different accents there are.
And just know as well that there's so

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:15.520
many different French speakers in Canada.
Today we're going to focus on Quebec French,

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:19.280
but if you like this video, I might make more.
Also, I just feel I need to share this,

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:23.920
but every person from Quebec that I've met
told me that French people from France often

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:27.920
found their accent funny — which seriously
makes me feel a bit sad and disappointed.

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:33.520
Because Quebec French and other French accents
are 100% valid ways of speaking French to me.

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:37.760
And also, I want to add that it's not just
these people. Some French people love making

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:42.480
fun of accents. Like, I personally remember when I
moved to Paris from the South of France all these

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:46.560
years ago, there were a few Parisians who made
fun of me for having a southern accent. Like,

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:51.600
I remember I couldn't say a word without having
those people laughing at me. And I think even 15

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:55.440
years later, I'm still a bit salty about it.
I just wish people would approach differences

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:59.120
with a bit more curiosity, because it
just makes life way more interesting.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:03.200
All right, one thing I did know about Quebec
is that they have so many expressions that

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:07.680
are different from the ones we would
use in France. So let's check them out.

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:12.734
In France, this would mean like you fell.

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:30.800
Yeah, I knew this one as well. There's a few
that kind of mean something different in France,

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:35.440
but I think I would understand in context.
Also, I love "magasiner" because in France,

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:38.080
we would use a mixture of
French and English — like,

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:42.240
we would say " faire du shoppingshopping"
with a French accent. And unlike Quebec,

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:48.160
in France we're really famous for our very weird
use of English. Like, instead of translating movie

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:52.320
titles, we give them a weird English title
and then we say it with a big French accent.

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:57.120
Like in France, this movie is not "The
Hangover" — it's "Very Bad Trip." But in Quebec,

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:02.320
it's "Lendemain de veille," which sounds so
much nicer in my opinion. And Quebec seems

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:06.160
to translate so many more things into
French, which, in my personal opinion,

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:11.680
is really nice — because I cannot stand the bad
faux English in France anymore, especially in ads.

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:16.640
Like seriously, they can be ridiculous sometimes.
In any case, I really love all these differences.

