So one thing I knew about Quebec is that they

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:23.360
really go the extra mile to promote the
French language, and sometimes it's done

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:27.120
in ways not everyone agrees with. And
we will not really get into that today,

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:31.840
because this is supposed to be a fun and light
discovery of the language and the culture.

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:36.720
But one way I really found interesting though
is the French classes that are offered to new

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:41.840
immigrants who don't yet speak French. And here
I found this amazing documentary that follows a

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:46.880
group of people from all over the world who just
arrived recently in Quebec and are trying to learn

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:51.935
French in 6 months — which can help them with
job opportunities or getting permanent residency.

00:07:05.401 --> 00:07:10.160
Okay, so what I love about this class is that
it's very concrete. Like, it's not just grammar

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:14.800
and vocab—it's all the situations you would
encounter in your daily life if you had just moved

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:20.560
to a country. So like, going to the restaurant,
finding an apartment, finding work. And that's so

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:24.960
important because moving to a new place when you
don't speak the language can be very intimidating.

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:38.080
This teacher seems awesome. I don't want
to spoil the ending of the documentary,

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:42.320
but he's doing such a good job at
preparing them for everyday situations,

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:46.720
which is truly what you need if you're learning
French in six months instead of focusing on

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:50.080
trying to be perfect. And I found that so
interesting, that there's different programs

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:54.320
to help people learn the language when they come
to Quebec. And they also have official programs

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:58.720
for people who already are French speakers.
Like, in France, there's often some official

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:03.120
meetings that are organized by Quebec to
promote French immigration to Quebec. And

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:07.680
I know that there's a lot of young French
people who want to come to Quebec—Montreal

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:12.160
in particular—because they make it so easy for
French people to come and go there, with even

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:17.200
having a specific type of visa which makes it
easier for French speakers to come to Quebec.

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:22.000
I personally know a lot of French people who have
spent at least six months in Quebec, either for

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:27.680
work, for an internship, for their studies. And
it's often seen by French people as an easier

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:33.360
way to discover a new culture since we speak the
same language. But it's not always easy, as we're

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:38.000
going to see in this next video, which is a French
documentary about French people who are coming to

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:43.520
Quebec in search of good job opportunities. And
here we're following this guy who is applying to

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:48.734
different positions in cafés and receives feedback
on cultural differences and how to find work.

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:11.360
Okay, so this is so interesting that he's
receiving that as feedback, because there

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:18.000
are so many English words at work in French.
Like we said: meeting, email, customer success,

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:21.701
sales—all of these words, we say them with a
French accent. All right, let's keep going.

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:40.960
It seems to be quite a big cultural
difference. It's true that in France,

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:45.440
as a society, we're still very
formal. We value education a lot,

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:50.320
and here it seems clear that—at least for this
guy—there seems to be a difference in culture.

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:54.000
And even if you speak the same language,
you still need to adapt. It's not because

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:58.720
you can understand what someone says that you
don't need to make an effort to adapt to their

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:02.400
culture when you're coming to their country.
All right, now let's see it from a Quebec

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:06.480
perspective and watch this video
by a French creator that I love,

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:10.468
who went around and asked people from
Quebec what they thought of French people.

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:38.560
This is so true. French people love
to complain and critique stuff.

00:10:46.734 --> 00:10:54.240
Also true. I feel personally attacked by that.
Yeah, that's also a thing. French people...

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:58.160
I don't know if that's the case—if you're
from Quebec, let me know. Do you say we're

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:05.440
cousins as well? We always say that Quebec
people are cousins. We say "nos cousins québécois". So let me know,

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:09.668
if you're from Quebec—are we? Are
we cousins? But I do feel this way.

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:29.600
Loved those rapid fire questions, they were
so funny. And you know, I think it's the

00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:36.095
perfect way to end that video. It was so fun to
discover the language and the culture of Quebec. And now I just need to travel here!



